t(20, 440, """How did the Arabs struggle over the rule of the valley of Bakkah, which is part of (Mecca the Honoured), before Islam? It is good for us to know our history. Would people not desire a city like Mecca, to settle in, to dwell in and to rule? So we do not blame those who struggled over it before Islam, for it is a jewel and a pearl guarded by Allah, our Lord, the Most Merciful. What follows is the story of the tribe of Khuza'ah seizing Mecca the Honoured and wresting it from Jurhum; and Jurhum, whom Hajar (peace be upon her) had allowed to settle in the valley, had expelled Quraysh from Mecca and taken it over, and Quraysh had scattered around Mecca and across the Arabian Peninsula after being expelled from its original homeland (Mecca). Whoever of Quraysh is of the offspring of Isma'il (peace be upon him) is from Fihr ibn Malik ibn al-Nadr ibn Kinanah .. The upshot is that Jurhum expelled those whose forefathers had allowed it to settle in the valley. It must be pointed out that all this was before Islam, when people were guided neither by the light of prophethood nor by a Book to protect them from error, and grasped of what is right only the little that had remained in their consciences generation after generation. When Islam came it made brothers of Jurhum, Khuza'ah and all the tribes, then made brothers of all peoples; then, Allah Most High willing, in its coming brotherhood it will make brothers of the nations, in the last of the stages of brotherhood .. As for the text that follows, it is on the one hand for the pleasure of history and on the other for the lesson. For Jurhum became corrupt, so Allah Most High replaced it with Khuza'ah; then Khuza'ah became corrupt, so Allah Most High replaced it with Quraysh, returning the original people of the land to their city to rule it themselves for the first time, after their numbers had grown and they had become fit to be a complete tribe descended from Isma'il (peace be upon him). This had not been the case with Hajar (peace be upon her) and her son Isma'il (peace be upon him), as only two individuals at the very beginning of life in Mecca; for Jurhum, whom Hajar (peace be upon her) permitted to settle in the valley, was a tribe at that time, while Quraysh did not yet exist .. This story is one of the stories of replacement that ought to guide people to this: the basis of control over the land is doing what is right.

Before Khuza'ah seized Mecca and expelled Jurhum from it, the tribe of Jurhum was the one in control of Mecca and the Sanctuary, after the Prophet Isma'il and his mother Hajar allowed them to dwell in the valley.

With time:
Jurhum became corrupt
They wronged the people
They violated the Sanctuary
They devoured the wealth of the Kaaba
So their standing among the Arabs weakened
And so people began to resent them.

Khuza'ah belongs to al-Azd; their origin is from Yemen.
After the collapse of the Ma'rib Dam, al-Azd scattered across the Peninsula, and among them was:

Amr ibn Luhayy al-Khuza'i, chief of the tribe of Khuza'ah
He reached Mecca
Learned of the weakness of most of Jurhum
And found an opportunity to take control of the Sanctuary

Although Khuza'ah was not of the line of Isma'il, it was strong militarily and in population.

1. Jurhum had collapsed from within
Jurhum split and its administration became corrupt, so they grew weak.

2. Khuza'ah came with great manpower
With the waves of migration from Yemen, their strength in numbers grew.

3. The outbreak of war between Khuza'ah and Jurhum
Battles took place between:
Khuza'ah, led by Amr ibn Luhayy
Jurhum, led by Mudad ibn Amr al-Jurhumi
And they ended with the victory of Khuza'ah.

4. The expulsion of Jurhum from Mecca
Jurhum left humiliated, so that the Jurhumi poet said:
As though Jurhum had never dwelt here yesterday...
And no men of glory among them had lived in the Sanctuary""")

t(21, 50, """During their departure they buried the treasures of the Kaaba for a long time, and it is said that Mudad ibn Amr buried the well of Zamzam itself.

Khuza'ah ruled Mecca for about a full 500 years.

It was responsible for:
The siqayah (providing water)
The rifadah (providing food)
Administration of the Sanctuary
Organizing the Hajj season

But it committed great errors under the leadership of Amr ibn Luhayy, the most important being:
Bringing idols into Mecca
Changing the religion of Ibrahim
Introducing deviant rites

Where was Quraysh during Khuza'ah's rule?
At this stage Quraysh was not spread through Mecca.
Quraysh was scattered on the outskirts of the Hijaz, because:
Jurhum had expelled them from Mecca long before
And Khuza'ah inherited this exclusion
That is why Quraysh was called Quraysh al-Zawahir (the tribes near Mecca, not inside it).

How did rule return to Quraysh, and how was Khuza'ah expelled?
Centuries later, in the era of:
⭐ Qusayy ibn Kilab, leader of Quraysh and its first power
He formed an alliance with:
The tribe of Kinanah
The tribe of Banu Bakr ibn Abd Manat

And after the battle of the war of Khuza'ah and Banu Bakr
Qusayy ibn Kilab wrested away:
The sidanah (custodianship of the Kaaba)
The rifadah
The hijabah (keeping of the Kaaba's door)
Dar al-Nadwah
Political rule

And rule returned to Quraysh al-Bitah, inside Mecca""")

