t(25, 115, """Mecca the Honoured, our Mighty Lord increased it in honour when our Prophet ﷺ was living in it .. This is how it was""")

t(25, 1225, """Click on the picture .. The picture shows what the Noble Meccan Sanctuary and the houses of those around it were like in the time of the Prophet ﷺ .. This is the first picture in history based on sources and documents .. the product of research work by a Kuwaiti researcher that went on for 15 years""")

t(25, 2380, """Quraysh was made up of clans, and Mecca the Honoured was, as we say today, a world city; except that it was a world city on the scale of the Arabian Peninsula only, not on the scale of the whole world or even the whole East. So it is more correct to call it regional, though there were those who came to stay in it from outside the Arabian Peninsula .. Among its slaves there were Abyssinians and Romans too, especially the slave women, contrary to what we used to think, that they were all Abyssinians only .. The tribes came to it on pilgrimage every year .. And people stayed in it for other than the Hajj, for all year round merchants came to it and in it were concluded""")

t(26, 60, """the deals .. Because of all this, the people in Mecca, other than its visitors, were divided into four categories .. The citizens, in today's terms .. that is, the Qurayshis, the sons of the various clans of Quraysh .. Among these citizens, apart from the visitor and the pilgrim, lived 3 categories of people .. The first category were the slaves, and we all know them. The second category of Mecca's residents were the allies (hulafa'); they were not slaves but free people who had settled in Mecca, having come from outside it as migrants, and had decided to live in it among its people (Quraysh). But in search of safety for the family, husband, wife and children, and in the absence of a state that protects and a socio-political order that regulates the law, these new expatriates, or migrants, or newcomers to Mecca needed to secure their lives against any dangers. So the free expatriate head of the family would go to one of the clans of Quraysh and agree with them, in an agreement declared before the people of Mecca, to be their ally and they his allies, in return for their protecting him from any attack on him or his family; and in return he would support them if anyone attacked their branch (the clan they belong to) .. And they treated him as a free man, in a manner very similar to their dealings with their own sons and kinsfolk ..
As for the third category, they were the mawali. The mawali are slaves whose masters freed them, so they became free; but by force of custom they became obliged, not by their own choice, to be clients (mawali) of the former masters and of the former master's clan. So if the clan was exposed to danger, the former slave who had been freed was obliged to defend his former master's clan; thus his becoming a mawla was not optional but compulsory in custom and in the law of manumission.""")

h(26, 900, """Tribal relations in Mecca before Islam: The tribe → Son of the tribe (Qurayshi) → Slave / Ally / Mawla (freed)""")

t(26, 1660, """So that people do not get things mixed up in the Great Third (War) .. This is the smoke of a satellite, not the smoke of the sign of the Smoke, which is one of the ten major signs of the Hour .. The sign of the Smoke is a divine sign and not a human act!""")
